The mitigation transposes, and in v0.3 it is no longer this paper's to specify. The resolution paper derives an anchor set — a fixed collection of claim pairs with independently established relations, re-measured every pass — from a stronger requirement than experimental control: its two measures are purely relative, so the relation surface has no origin, and without anchoring the layer is confined to comparing pairs that already co-retrieve.
This paper inherits it and adds one obligation. Every pass is evaluated against the anchor set as well as against the live stream, and divergence between them is the loop's own environmental contribution. Anchor coordinates drifting while the anchors themselves have not changed is an instrument fault at first order, not a second-order finding, and the tier must not attribute it to its own operation.
That the same mechanism is required for calibration at first order and for control at second order is worth noting rather than passing over: an instrument that cannot place distant pairs and an experiment that cannot separate improvement from drift need the same fixed reference, for the same underlying reason.
